{
    "title": "Launching the Genesis Mission",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "November 28, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-28/pdf/2025-21665.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House has launched the Genesis Mission, a national push to use artificial intelligence to speed up scientific discovery. Led by the Department of Energy, it will build a secure, shared system that unites federal scientific data, supercomputers, and research tools to tackle major challenges in fields like manufacturing, biotechnology, critical materials, nuclear energy, quantum science, and semiconductors.\n\nIn practice, this means creating a common platform where researchers can access powerful computing, standardized datasets, and software that can help generate ideas, run simulations, and even guide automated experiments. The plan sets short timelines to map available computing resources, pull together initial data and models, assess robotic lab capabilities, and show an early working system. A federal science council will coordinate agencies, while new partnerships will invite universities, companies, and other collaborators to contribute data, tools, and expertise under strict security, privacy, and intellectual property protections. The order also calls for fellowships and internships to train students in artificial intelligence\u2013enabled research, and for annual reporting on progress, outcomes, and needs.\n\nWho stands to benefit? Scientists at national labs, universities, and startups could gain faster paths from ideas to results. Companies that build advanced computing, data systems, materials, and tools may find new opportunities through public\u2011private collaborations and funding programs. Students and early\u2011career researchers could get access to cutting\u2011edge resources and training. If it works as intended, the broader public could see quicker advances in energy, manufacturing, health\u2011related technologies, and other areas tied to economic growth and national security.\n\nWho might be negatively affected? Smaller institutions and independent researchers could find it harder to participate if access rules, costs, or security requirements are too high. Sharing and integrating large datasets raises real concerns about privacy, ownership, and fair use, even with safeguards. Tight security controls and export restrictions may limit international collaboration and slow open science. Centralizing so much computing and data in one system can create cybersecurity and supply\u2011chain risks. And as research becomes more automated, parts of the scientific workforce could face disruption without clear plans for reskilling and inclusion. Finally, the scope of the mission depends on funding and sustained coordination, which can be challenging over time.\n\nOverall, the Genesis Mission is an ambitious bet that a unified, secure national system for artificial intelligence\u2013driven research can accelerate discovery. Its impact will hinge on execution: broad, equitable access; strong protections; responsible partnerships; and measurable results.",
